### Structs in C++ vs C

In C++, `struct` and `class` are **virtually identical** with only one difference:
- In a `struct`, members and base classes are **public by default**.
- In a `class`, members and base classes are **private by default**.

C++ structs can have constructors, destructors, member functions, operator overloads, and inheritance:

```cpp
struct Point {
    int x{0}, y{0}; // Default member initialization (C++11)

    double distanceToOrigin() const {
        return std::hypot(x, y);
    }
};
```

### Collections of Structs using `std::vector`:
```cpp
std::vector<Point> points = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}, {5, 6}};
std::sort(points.begin(), points.end(), [](const Point &a, const Point &b) {
    return a.x < b.x;
});
```

